(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-2-circulatory-physiology-1-quiz"]={"title":"Circulatory Physiology I — Physiology Exam 2","category":"Physiology","questions":[{"q":"What is the primary functional role of arterioles in the circulation?","choices":["Low-pressure reservoir holding the majority of total circulating blood volume","Control site for blood flow and the major resistance site of the circulation","Primary site of water and solute exchange between blood and interstitial fluid","Primary site of gas exchange between alveolar air and pulmonary capillary blood"],"answer":1,"exp":"Arterioles are the control site for blood flow and the major resistance site of the circulation, in contrast to capillaries (exchange site) and large veins/venules (low-pressure reservoir). — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What is the primary functional role of capillaries?","choices":["The primary site controlling total peripheral vascular resistance","The major low-pressure reservoir holding most of the circulating blood","The major site of water and solute exchange between blood and tissues","The primary conduit delivering oxygenated blood at high systolic pressure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Capillaries are the major site of water and solute exchange between blood and tissues, in contrast to the aorta/large arteries (high-pressure transport) and arterioles (resistance control). — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which vascular component has by far the largest total cross-sectional area, and what does this mean for blood velocity there?","choices":["The aorta (about 2.5 cm² in cross-sectional area), with the fastest overall blood velocity","The capillaries (about 1000x the aorta's cross-sectional area), with the slowest blood velocity","The venae cavae (about 8 cm² combined), with a moderately fast venous return velocity","All vascular components have roughly equal cross-sectional area throughout the entire circulatory tree"],"answer":1,"exp":"Capillaries have by far the largest total cross-sectional area (about 2500 cm², roughly 1000 times that of the aorta at 2.5 cm²); since velocity = flow/cross-sectional area, blood velocity is slowest in the capillaries, allowing time for exchange. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Where is the majority of total blood volume located within the circulation?","choices":["In the veins (about 60%), which serve as a blood reservoir","In the arteries (about 15%), which serve as a pressure conduit","In the capillaries (about 5%), where exchange primarily occurs","In the pulmonary circulation (about 60%), acting as a reservoir"],"answer":0,"exp":"The majority of blood volume (about 60%) is located in the veins, which serve as a blood reservoir, with total blood volume in humans being about 5 liters. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"How does blood pressure change across the circulatory system, and where does the largest pressure drop occur?","choices":["Pressure is high in the arterial tree and low on the venous side, with the largest pressure drop occurring across the arteriolar-capillary junction","Pressure is constant throughout the entire circulation, since vascular resistance is distributed evenly across every vessel type","The largest pressure drop occurs across the venae cavae themselves, just before blood returns to the right atrium","Pressure is highest in the veins and lowest in the arteries, reversing the normal direction of blood flow throughout"],"answer":0,"exp":"Blood pressure is high in the arterial tree and progressively lower on the venous side, with the largest single pressure drop occurring across the arteriolar-capillary junction — reflecting the arterioles' role as the major resistance site. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"According to the basic theory of circulatory function, what mainly controls cardiac output, and is arterial pressure controlled by the same mechanism?","choices":["Arterial pressure and cardiac output are controlled by an identical single mechanism, centered entirely on carotid and aortic baroreceptor reflexes","Cardiac output has no relationship to tissue needs whatsoever, being fixed entirely by the intrinsic SA node firing rate alone","Cardiac output is mainly controlled by local tissue blood flow needs; arterial pressure is controlled independently of local flow or cardiac output control","Local tissue flow has no bearing on cardiac output at all, which is instead set entirely by central venous pressure alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Blood flow to tissues is controlled in relation to tissue needs, and cardiac output is mainly controlled by local tissue flow; arterial pressure, by contrast, is controlled independently of either local blood flow control or cardiac output control. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"According to Q = ΔP/R, what two factors determine blood flow through a vessel?","choices":["Blood flow is independent of pressure and resistance, determined only by total blood volume","Only the diameter of the heart's chambers, unrelated to any pressure gradient along the vessel","The pressure difference (ΔP) between the two ends of the vessel and the resistance (R) of the vessel","Only vessel length and blood viscosity, with no contribution from the pressure gradient"],"answer":2,"exp":"Flow (Q) through a blood vessel is determined by the pressure difference (ΔP, or P1 − P2) between the two ends of the vessel and the resistance (R) of the vessel, following Q = ΔP/R. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"What is laminar flow, and what velocity profile does it create?","choices":["Laminar flow creates a completely uniform velocity profile across the entire vessel, with no difference between center and wall","Laminar flow only occurs in veins, never in arteries, due to the much lower blood pressures found on the venous side of circulation","Blood flows randomly with no streamline pattern at all, creating a flat velocity profile identical at both the center and near the wall","Blood flows in streamlines with each layer remaining the same distance from the wall, creating a parabolic profile where central blood velocity is greater than near the wall"],"answer":3,"exp":"Laminar flow occurs when blood flows in streamlines, with each layer remaining the same distance from the vessel wall; this creates a parabolic velocity profile where blood in the center of the vessel moves faster than blood near the outer edge. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"How is vascular resistance calculated?","choices":["R = ∆P/Q (pressure difference divided by blood flow)","R = ∆P × Q","R = Q/∆P","Resistance cannot be calculated from pressure and flow"],"answer":0,"exp":"Resistance (R) is calculated by dividing the pressure difference (ΔP) between two points in a vessel by the vessel's blood flow (Q): R = ΔP/Q. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"In a parallel arrangement of vascular resistances (as in the peripheral circulation), how does total resistance compare to the resistance of the single lowest-resistance vessel?","choices":["Total resistance is less than the resistance of the vessel with the lowest resistance","Total resistance equals the sum of all individual resistances added together in sequence","Parallel arrangement has no measurable effect on total resistance compared to a single vessel","Total resistance is always exactly equal to the average of all individual vessel resistances"],"answer":0,"exp":"In a parallel arrangement (as in the peripheral circulation, allowing independent control of flow to different tissues), total resistance is actually less than the resistance of the single vessel with the lowest resistance. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"How does vascular conductance change with vessel radius, according to Poiseuille's law?","choices":["Conductance is inversely proportional to the square of the radius","Conductance increases in proportion to the fourth power of the radius","Conductance increases in proportion to the square of the radius","Conductance is independent of radius, depending only on vessel length"],"answer":1,"exp":"Vascular conductance is very sensitive to changes in vessel diameter — it increases in proportion to the fourth power of the radius (Q = πΔPr⁴/8ηL), meaning small changes in radius produce large changes in flow. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"If a vessel narrows to half its original radius, by approximately how much does resistance increase?","choices":["16-fold","No change in resistance","2-fold","4-fold"],"answer":0,"exp":"Because resistance is inversely proportional to the fourth power of the radius, a vessel that narrows by half increases resistance approximately 16-fold. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 91"},{"q":"How much more distensible are veins compared to arteries?","choices":["Veins are 8 times more distensible than arteries","Veins and arteries have identical distensibility","Arteries are more distensible than veins","Veins are only slightly (10%) more distensible"],"answer":0,"exp":"Vascular distensibility (the fractional increase in volume for each mm Hg rise in pressure) is about 8 times greater in veins than in arteries, reflecting their thinner, more compliant walls. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"How does the capacitance of veins compare to that of arteries?","choices":["Veins have about 8 times the capacitance of arteries","The capacitance of veins is about 24 times that of arteries","Arteries have roughly twice the capacitance of veins","Veins and arteries differ in capacitance by only about 10%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Vascular capacitance (capacitance ≈ distensibility × volume) is about 24 times greater in veins than in arteries, allowing veins to store large quantities of blood with relatively small pressure changes and function as a reservoir. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"How is pulse pressure defined and calculated?","choices":["Pulse pressure is the difference between systolic and diastolic pressure (e.g., 120 − 80 = 40 mm Hg)","Pulse pressure is the sum of systolic and diastolic pressure divided by two (e.g., 120 + 80 ÷ 2 = 100 mm Hg)","Pulse pressure is the ratio of systolic to diastolic pressure (e.g., 120 ÷ 80 = 1.5)","Pulse pressure equals mean arterial pressure minus diastolic pressure (e.g., 93 − 80 = 13 mm Hg)"],"answer":0,"exp":"Pulse pressure is the difference between systolic pressure (the height of the pressure pulse, e.g., 120 mm Hg) and diastolic pressure (the lowest point, e.g., 80 mm Hg), giving a typical pulse pressure of about 40 mm Hg. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What two factors determine the degree of damping of pulse pressure as it travels into smaller peripheral arteries?","choices":["The heart rate and the total blood volume within the systemic arterial circulation","The resistance of the small vessels and arterioles, and the compliance of the larger vessels","The elasticity of the venous system and the total peripheral resistance of the capillary beds","The stroke volume ejected by the left ventricle and the resistance of the aortic valve"],"answer":1,"exp":"The degree of damping of pulse pressure in the peripheral arteries is proportional to the resistance of the small vessels/arterioles and the compliance of the larger vessels; pulsation intensity progressively decreases in smaller arteries. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What characteristic pulse pressure pattern is seen with patent ductus arteriosus?","choices":["Low systolic pressure and elevated diastolic pressure, resulting in an unusually narrow pulse pressure","Low diastolic pressure and high systolic pressure, resulting in a very high pulse pressure","Both systolic and diastolic pressures fall equally, leaving pulse pressure essentially unchanged","Systolic and diastolic pressures both rise in tandem, keeping the pulse pressure roughly normal"],"answer":1,"exp":"Patent ductus arteriosus is associated with low diastolic pressure and high systolic pressure, resulting in a very high pulse pressure — reflecting the abnormal runoff of blood from the aorta into the pulmonary circulation during diastole. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"What are Korotkoff sounds, and how are they used to measure systolic and diastolic pressure?","choices":["Continuous swishing sounds produced by turbulent blood flow in the brachial artery that persist at any cuff pressure above venous pressure, unrelated to systolic or diastolic values","Tapping sounds that begin when cuff pressure reaches systolic pressure, become muffled near diastolic pressure, and then disappear — the basis of the auscultatory method","Sounds that begin below diastolic pressure, grow louder as cuff pressure falls further, and disappear only once cuff pressure reaches zero","High-pitched continuous sounds heard only while the cuff is being actively inflated, disappearing immediately once cuff inflation is stopped, regardless of arterial pressure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Korotkoff sounds are heard via the auscultatory method: as cuff pressure falls to systolic pressure, tapping sounds begin (blood starts flowing through the partially compressed artery); as cuff pressure approaches diastolic pressure, the sounds become muffled and then disappear. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Besides the veins in general, which specific organs serve as important blood reservoirs, and which one specifically reserves red blood cells?","choices":["The lungs, kidneys, and skeletal muscle vasculature serve as reservoirs, with the kidneys specializing in platelet storage","The lungs alone serve as the body's major blood reservoir, capable of shifting large volumes into the systemic circulation","The spleen, liver, large abdominal veins, and venous plexus serve as reservoirs, with the spleen also serving as a special reservoir for red blood cells","Only the liver and kidneys serve as significant blood reservoirs, with the liver additionally storing white blood cell reserves"],"answer":2,"exp":"The spleen, liver, large abdominal veins, and the venous plexus serve as blood reservoirs, transferring blood into the arterial system as needed to maintain arterial pressure; the spleen additionally serves as a special reservoir specifically for red blood cells. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What is central venous pressure, and what is its normal value?","choices":["The pressure in the left atrium, normally about 2 to 5 mm Hg at rest","The pressure in the pulmonary artery, normally about 25 mm Hg systolic","The pressure in the right atrium, normally 0 mm Hg (but can rise to 20-30 mm Hg)","The pressure in the aorta, normally about 100 mm Hg mean arterial pressure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Central venous pressure is the pressure in the right atrium, normally about 0 mm Hg, though it can rise as high as 20-30 mm Hg under certain conditions (e.g., heart failure, volume overload). — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Which of the following would tend to increase right atrial pressure (central venous pressure)?","choices":["Decreased blood volume, decreased venous tone, constriction of arterioles, or increased cardiac function","Constriction of arterioles alone, with no contribution from venous tone or blood volume","Increased blood volume, increased venous tone, dilation of arterioles, or decreased cardiac function","Decreased blood volume, dilated veins, constricted arterioles, or improved cardiac pumping ability"],"answer":2,"exp":"Right atrial pressure is regulated by the balance between the heart's ability to pump blood out and venous blood inflow; it is increased by increased blood volume, increased venous tone, dilation of arterioles (increasing venous return), or decreased cardiac function (reduced pumping ability). — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"How high can venous pressure rise in the feet due to the weight of blood in a standing person, and what mechanism helps keep it lower?","choices":["Only about 25 mm Hg maximum, fully offset by baroreceptor-driven arteriolar constriction","Up to 40 mm Hg due to gravity; arterial vasoconstriction alone keeps leg venous pressure controlled","Up to 90 mm Hg due to gravity; venous valves and the \"venous pump\" help keep leg venous pressure relatively low","Up to 200 mm Hg due to gravity, with no mechanism able to lower it while standing still"],"answer":2,"exp":"The weight of blood in the vessels can cause venous pressure in the feet to rise as high as 90 mm Hg while standing; venous valves and the \"venous pump\" (from skeletal muscle contraction) help maintain relatively low venous pressure in the legs despite this gravitational effect. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"What are the three ways arterial pressure (= cardiac output × total peripheral resistance) can be increased?","choices":["By dilating arterioles (decreasing TPR), dilating large veins (decreasing venous return), or directly decreasing heart rate and contractility","By constricting arterioles (increasing TPR), constricting large veins (increasing venous return/cardiac output), or directly increasing heart rate and contractility","By constricting the arterioles alone; changes in heart rate, contractility, or venous tone have no measurable effect on arterial pressure","By simultaneously dilating all arterioles and veins throughout the body, which paradoxically raises both resistance and cardiac output"],"answer":1,"exp":"Arterial pressure = cardiac output × total peripheral resistance, and can be increased by constricting arterioles (increasing TPR), constricting large veins (increasing venous return and thus cardiac output), or directly increasing cardiac output via increased heart rate and contractility. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"Where is the vasomotor center (VMC) located, and what three functional areas does it comprise?","choices":["Located bilaterally in the hypothalamus and midbrain; comprises only a vasoconstrictor and a vasodilator area","Located bilaterally in the reticular substance of the medulla and lower pons; comprises a vasoconstrictor area, vasodilator area, and sensory area","Located within the cerebellar vermis; comprises a pressor area, a depressor area, and a cardioinhibitory area","Located exclusively in the thoracolumbar spinal cord; comprises a single undifferentiated vasoconstrictor area"],"answer":1,"exp":"The vasomotor center (VMC) is located bilaterally in the reticular substance of the medulla and the lower third of the pons, and is composed of a vasoconstrictor area, a vasodilator area, and a sensory area. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 100"},{"q":"What is \"vasomotor tone,\" and what area of the VMC generates it?","choices":["A complete, maximal state of vasoconstriction produced only transiently during acute hemorrhage or severe pain","A brief, reflexive burst of vasodilation triggered only by activation of the baroreceptor reflex during standing","A partial, ongoing state of vasoconstriction maintained by continuous sympathetic vasoconstrictor tone from the VMC's vasoconstrictor area","A phenomenon produced exclusively by parasympathetic vagal fibers innervating the sinoatrial node and AV node"],"answer":2,"exp":"Vasomotor tone is the partial state of contraction maintained in blood vessels by continuous, ongoing sympathetic vasoconstrictor signals transmitted from the vasoconstrictor area of the VMC — this baseline \"sympathetic vasoconstrictor tone\" maintains basal blood pressure. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 101"},{"q":"What is the neurotransmitter released by vasoconstrictor nerves under VMC control, and how does epinephrine from the adrenal medulla differ in its vascular effects?","choices":["Epinephrine is the sole vasoconstrictor neurotransmitter released from sympathetic nerve terminals, with norepinephrine playing no vascular role","Dopamine is the vasoconstrictor neurotransmitter; norepinephrine from the adrenal medulla causes only vasodilation via beta-1 receptors","Acetylcholine is the vasoconstrictor neurotransmitter for sympathetic nerves; epinephrine constricts vessels solely through beta-1 receptors","Norepinephrine is the vasoconstrictor neurotransmitter; epinephrine can also dilate vessels via a potent beta-2 receptor effect, in addition to constricting via alpha receptors"],"answer":3,"exp":"Norepinephrine is the neurotransmitter for sympathetic vasoconstrictor nerves; the adrenal medulla secretes both epinephrine and norepinephrine, which constrict vessels via alpha-adrenergic receptors, but epinephrine can also dilate vessels through a potent beta-2 receptor effect. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"How do the roles of the sympathetic and parasympathetic nervous systems differ in circulatory control?","choices":["Both systems act primarily through the same vasoconstrictor fibers innervating arterioles, producing nearly identical effects on resistance","The parasympathetic system controls vascular resistance throughout the body, while the sympathetic system regulates only heart rate","The sympathetic system is important in control of the circulation broadly; the parasympathetic system is mainly important in regulating heart rate via the vagus nerve","The sympathetic system has no role beyond regulating heart rate, while the parasympathetic system controls vascular resistance broadly"],"answer":2,"exp":"The sympathetic nervous system is important in control of the circulation generally (including vascular resistance), while the parasympathetic nervous system is mainly important in regulating heart rate via the vagus nerve. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"Which vessels are NOT innervated by sympathetic nerve fibers?","choices":["Large arteries, large veins, and the coronary sinus","The heart, the large veins, and the pulmonary arterioles","Capillaries, precapillary sphincters, and some meta-arterioles","Small arteries, arterioles, and the postcapillary venules"],"answer":2,"exp":"Sympathetic nerve fibers innervate all vessels except capillaries, precapillary sphincters, and some meta-arterioles; innervation of small arteries and arterioles allows sympathetic nerves to increase vascular resistance, and large veins and the heart are also sympathetically innervated. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"In which circulatory beds is the distribution of sympathetic vasoconstrictor fibers greatest, and where is it least potent?","choices":["Greatest in the skeletal muscle and lungs; least potent in the kidneys and spleen","Vasoconstrictor fibers are entirely absent from the kidneys, gut, and skin","Distributed equally across all vascular beds, including the coronary and cerebral circulations","Greatest in kidneys, gut, spleen, and skin; less potent in the coronary circulation and brain"],"answer":3,"exp":"Sympathetic vasoconstrictor fiber distribution is greatest in the kidneys, gut, spleen, and skin, but is less potent in the coronary circulation and the brain — protecting these vital organs from excessive constriction during sympathetic activation. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 98"},{"q":"What is the normal overall blood flow rate in the circulation of an adult, which is equivalent to cardiac output?","choices":["5 L/min","1 L/min","50 L/min","15 L/min"],"answer":0,"exp":"The overall flow in the circulation of an adult is about 5 L/min, which is equivalent to the cardiac output — the same value as total blood volume, meaning the entire blood volume circulates roughly once per minute at rest. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 18"}]};
